WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions of "ATP in Use" by Drew Berry, wehi.tv, for HHMI BioInteractive, from the film's page on YouTube (youtu.be/vUC5njkOq7o).
Each cue is one line here, and mishearings in the captions are put right (plans/follow-the-molecule/captions.py lists them).

1
00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.150
Adenosine Triphosphate, or ATP is the molecule

2
00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.140
that powers all life on Earth.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.190
Energy from ATP is required for essential activity,

4
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.150
including pumping ions across membranes,

5
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.220
in pulling apart the DNA helix.

6
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.920
The ATP molecule is made of three main structures;

7
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.400
an adenine nucleobase, a ribose sugar, and 3 phosphates.

8
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.320
Energy is released when the bond holding the end phosphate group

9
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:50.820
is broken converting ATP to ADP through a water consuming

10
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.020
hydrolysis reaction.

11
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.860
To stay alive, your cells depend on enzyme pumps

12
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:08.680
to maintain differences in ion concentration across membranes.

13
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.920
The flow of calcium ions controls muscle contraction,

14
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.580
nerve transmission, gene regulation, and cell death.

15
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.090
Because of enzyme pumps, the concentration

16
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:26.050
of calcium ions inside your living cells

17
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.320
is 10,000 times lower than outside.

18
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:38.300
The ATP hydrolysis reaction breaks the phosphate bond

19
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:43.940
releasing energy driving atoms to rearrange inside the enzyme,

20
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.900
transforming its shape.

21
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.420
With each transformation channels

22
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.070
inside the enzyme open and close sending ions

23
00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:01.190
in opposite directions; hydrogen in and calcium out of the cell.

24
00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:18.360
DNA helicase is another type of ATP powered enzyme.

25
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.690
This mechanism is so critical for life

26
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:28.550
that 1% of your genetic code is for helicase enzymes.

27
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.760
Helicase enzymes are motor proteins

28
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.050
that move along the DNA double helix, mechanically

29
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:40.130
separating strands powered by energy from ATP.

30
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:49.330
The ring-shaped enzyme uses ATP hydrolysis to provide energy

31
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:55.540
for the mechanical separation of strands of the DNA helix.

32
00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.400
With each step of the enzyme, two pairs of DNA bases

33
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.620
are separated requiring energy from two ATP molecules

34
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.710
around the ring.
